{"status": "done", "model": "gpt-6-astra", "effort": "high", "n_calls": 9, "api_errors": 0, "started": 1790554375.4366024, "usage": {"input": 42098, "output": 3997, "reasoning": 1791, "cached": 28928}, "items": [{"role": "developer", "content": "You are an autonomous software/ML engineering agent working in a Linux sandbox (Python 3.10 + numpy; no internet package installs). There is no human available: never ask questions, make reasonable decisions and keep working until the task is fully complete. Use the `shell` tool to inspect files, run experiments and edit code. When you are completely done, reply with a brief final summary and do not call any tool in that final message."}, {"role": "user", "content": "# Schedule the batch size for one fixed-token training run\n\n`solver-7b` is about to be pre-trained for a fixed number of tokens. The token budget is split into\n**40 equal segments**. Before each segment you choose the **batch size**; everything else in the recipe is\nfixed. Your goal is to finish the whole run in as few **GPU-seconds** as possible.\n\nTwo things set the cost of a segment:\n\n* the optimizer needs some number of steps to get through the segment's tokens, and that number depends on\n  the batch size you picked - a bigger batch needs fewer steps, but with diminishing returns;\n* each step costs `t_overhead + batch / throughput` seconds, both of which you are told exactly.\n\nThe team has never characterised the first part. What they do know: the corpus is not homogeneous - the data\nschedule switches source partway through the run, and nobody has checked what that does to the step counts.\n**The switch is scheduled per run.** Every run of this job gets its own switch point, drawn anywhere between\n40% and 80% of the way through the run (segments 16-32). The phase-2 corpus is assembled per run as well,\nso how much the switch costs, and how the cost grows after it, also vary run to run (by up to +-85%). Where\nand how the dev replica switched tells you nothing about the real run. The ramp *before* the switch is a\nproperty of the model and is the same every time.\n\n## The lab\n\nRun `/app/bin/lab spec` first. `lab profile` runs segments of a **dev replica** of the same run at batch\nsizes you choose, and reports the realised step count and seconds for each. Profiling costs exactly the\nseconds it simulates, out of a budget of **8.3 GPU-seconds** - about 2.2 times what the run itself takes -\nso a profile at a big batch is expensive. `lab evaluate` runs your current `/app/sched.py` end to end on a\ndev replica (cost 0.7, i.e. 9% of the budget).\n\n## Deliverable: `/app/sched.py`\n\nA module defining `run(env)`, run in a sandbox on **5 hidden runs** of the same service (see\n`/app/docs/sched_api.md`). It must call `env.run_segment(batch)` exactly 40 times. Your scheduler sees\neach segment's realised step count as it goes - it is a closed-loop controller, not a fixed list.\n\n## How it is graded (mechanically, against the simulator; only the file counts)\n\nLet `T` be your mean total seconds over the hidden runs. With `T_const` the time of the best possible\n**constant** batch size and `T_ref` the time of a well-tuned adaptive reference scheduler, both measured on\nthose same hidden runs,\n\n    s = (T_const - T) / (T_const - T_ref)\n\n1. **R0** - `sched.py` completes all 40 segments on every hidden run, without crashing and without an\n   out-of-range batch.\n2. **R1** - `s >= 0.90`.\n\nOn the dev replica the best constant batch takes about **3.78 s** and the reference scheduler about\n**3.47 s**. Both bars are re-measured on the hidden runs, so aim to beat the reference, not a number.\n\nOnly `/app/sched.py` is graded. Write it, check it with `lab evaluate`, then stop.\n"}, {"id": "fc_0b3323832328e832016ab9b109fa4087d1af1393a980e543e4", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"/app/bin/lab\",\"spec\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_cgbzXSYiRitJmaiksa4UyG12", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_cgbzXSYiRitJmaiksa4UyG12", "output": "{\"exit_code\": 0, \"duration_s\": 0.0, \"output\": \"{\\n \\\"ops\\\": {\\n  \\\"profile\\\": {\\n   \\\"args\\\": {\\n    \\\"start\\\": \\\"first segment index (0..39)\\\",\\n    \\\"segments\\\": \\\"how many consecutive segments to run (1..40)\\\",\\n    \\\"batches\\\": \\\"list of batch sizes, one per segment, each in [4, 8192]\\\"\\n   },\\n   \\\"cost\\\": \\\"the seconds it simulates (same formula as the real run)\\\",\\n   \\\"returns\\\": \\\"per segment: batch, realised steps, seconds\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"evaluate\\\": {\\n   \\\"args\\\": {},\\n   \\\"cost\\\": \\\"0.75 GPU-seconds\\\",\\n   \\\"returns\\\": \\\"total seconds of /app/sched.py on one dev replica, the best-constant-batch time, and the normalised score; error/stderr if it crashed\\\"\\n  }\\n },\\n \\\"deliverable\\\": \\\"/app/sched.py (see /app/docs/sched_api.md)\\\",\\n \\\"budget_total\\\": 8.32,\\n \\\"budget_left\\\": 8.32\\n}\\n\"}"}, {"id": "fc_0b3323832328e832016ab9b10dc22887d1b4766e55fca26095", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"bash\",\"-lc\",\"cat /app/docs/sched_api.md; ls -la /app; ls -la /app/bin\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_T1Islob40XGK3nAs2YZMMTbu", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_T1Islob40XGK3nAs2YZMMTbu", "output": "{\"exit_code\": 0, \"duration_s\": 0.0, \"output\": \"# Scheduler API\\n\\nYour file `/app/sched.py` must define `run(env)`. It is imported and called once per run inside a sandbox\\n(no network, no filesystem access beyond your own file, standard library only - **no numpy**). `print()`\\ngoes to stderr and is shown to you by `lab evaluate` when something goes wrong.\\n\\n    env.n_segments    # number of segments in the run\\n    env.b_min         # smallest batch you may request\\n    env.b_max         # largest batch you may request\\n    env.t_overhead    # fixed seconds per optimizer step\\n    env.throughput    # examples/second; per-step time is t_overhead + batch/throughput\\n\\n    r = env.run_segment(batch)\\n        # trains one segment at that batch size and returns\\n        #   {\\\"segment\\\": i, \\\"batch\\\": b, \\\"steps\\\": s, \\\"seconds\\\": t, \\\"segments_left\\\": k, \\\"done\\\": bool}\\n        # `steps` is what the segment actually needed - it is noisy.\\n        # Call this exactly env.n_segments times, in order. There is no undo.\\n\\n    env.elapsed()     # seconds used so far\\n\\nMinimal example (the current production schedule - a constant batch):\\n\\n    def run(env):\\n        for _ in range(env.n_segments):\\n            env.run_segment(512)\\ntotal 20\\ndrwx------  4 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 28 00:12 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 10 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat  260 Sep 28 00:13 ..\\n-rw-r--r--  1 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat   32 Sep 28 00:12 .lab_token\\ndrwx------  2 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 27 19:21 bin\\ndrwx------  2 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 27 19:21 docs\\n-rw-------  1 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat  224 Sep 27 19:21 sched.py\\ntotal 12\\ndrwx------ 2 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 27 19:21 .\\ndrwx------ 4 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 4096 Sep 28 00:12 ..\\n-rwx------ 1 dazzling-upbeat-fermat dazzling-upbeat-fermat 2375 Sep 27 19:21 lab\\n\"}"}, {"id": "fc_0b3323832328e832016ab9b111a4ac87d18055e5bb3deb8130", "type": "function_call", "arguments": "{\"command\":[\"bash\",\"-lc\",\"cat /app/sched.py; /app/bin/lab --help\"],\"timeout_ms\":150000,\"workdir\":\"/app\"}", "call_id": "call_nRXDmIw60jz87Zj6t6pTGTrJ", "name": "shell"}, {"type": "function_call_output", "call_id": "call_nRXDmIw60jz87Zj6t6pTGTrJ", "output": "{\"exit_code\": 0, \"duration_s\": 0.0, \"output\": \"# Current production schedule: a constant batch size,